Subject: [PATCH] mppdec: H.264 -- the broadcast's own timestamps, in microseconds

Measured on a DVB-S 1080i field-coded H.264 broadcast (TGRT BELGESEL HD) on
RK3588, from a recording that starts mid-GOP, as every channel opening does.

The timestamps MPP makes up for H.264 (use_mpp_pts) came out repeated after
the first key frame -- .253 .613 ... .893 eighteen times -- and the frames
behind them were dropped downstream as not after the one before: 150-480 ms
of picture missing in the first seconds of every channel. The software
decoder on the same stream gives .253 .333 .413 ...

Given the broadcast's PTS in microseconds, as for MPEG-2, h264d starts at
the key frame and times each field 20 ms apart: .373 .433 .453 .473 ...

---
--- a/gst/rockchipmpp/gstmppdec.c
+++ b/gst/rockchipmpp/gstmppdec.c
@@ -375,7 +375,7 @@
   GST_DEBUG_OBJECT (self, "setting format: %" GST_PTR_FORMAT, state->caps);
 
   /* The MPP m2vd's PTS is buggy */
-  if (self->mpp_type == MPP_VIDEO_CodingMPEG2) {
+  if (self->mpp_type == MPP_VIDEO_CodingMPEG2 || self->mpp_type == MPP_VIDEO_CodingAVC) {
     self->use_mpp_pts = FALSE;
 
     /* It is not, given the unit it reads: m2vd times each picture from its
@@ -386,7 +386,16 @@
      * and the matching below threw frames away: a broadcast's 576i played
      * 18 frames a second of 50. In microseconds, as ffmpeg's rkmppdec
      * gives it (AV_TIME_BASE), its times are the pictures' own, in display
-     * order, and each field its own; they are used as they come. */
+     * order, and each field its own; they are used as they come.
+     *
+     * H.264 the same: the timestamps MPP makes up for it, which the matching
+     * below has to guess frames by, come out repeated when a field-coded
+     * broadcast is joined mid-GOP -- the frames after the first key frame
+     * all timed alike (one time 18 times, TGRT BELGESEL HD, 1080i), dropped
+     * downstream as not after the one before: 150-480 ms of picture missing
+     * at every channel opening. Given the broadcast's own PTS in
+     * microseconds, h264d starts at the key frame and times every field
+     * 20 ms apart (measured 2026-10-30 on a recording of that channel). */
     self->pts_in_us = TRUE;
   }
 
